<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Saruhanids</h1><p class="st-meta">1300 CE – 1410 CE (approx.) · capital Manisa</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Turkish beylik of western Anatolia centred on Manisa, a partner in the 14th-century naval raids on the Aegean.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saruhan-bey">Saruhan Bey</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> commander, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manisa">Magnesia</a> (Manisa) from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> in 1313 and made it the capital of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beylik">beylik</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gediz-river">Hermus</a> valley, north of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/aydinids">Aydinids</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Saruhanids joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/aydinids">Aydinids</a> in raiding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> islands and the Greek mainland, and at times sent troops to help Byzantine factions, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-vi-kantakouzenos">John VI Kantakouzenos</a>. The fertile <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gediz-river">Gediz</a> plain made the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beylik">beylik</a> prosperous, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ishak-bey-of-saruhan">Ishak Bey</a> built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-mosque-of-manisa">Great Mosque of Manisa</a> in 1366-1367. The beylik also traded with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lordship-of-phocaea">Genoese Phocaea</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayezid-i">Bayezid I</a> annexed Saruhan in 1390. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> restored it after the battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ankara">Ankara</a> in 1402, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> absorbed it again around 1410, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manisa">Manisa</a> became the seat of Ottoman princes.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/saruhanids.jpg" alt="Saruhanids" width="320" height="248"><figcaption>Image: Alexander Keith Johnston · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:W._%26_A.K._Johnston._Asia_Minor._1911_A.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1300 CE – 1410 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Manisa</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Saruhan Bey, Ishak Bey, Hizir Shah</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Turkish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Saruhanid Beylik, Saruhanoğulları</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1355&amp;focus=saruhanids" aria-label="Saruhanids on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5361 792 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Saruhanids lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5681 1048L5709 970M5681 1048L6582 1190"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5709 970h0M6582 1190h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5681 1048h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5681 1048h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Manisa. <a href="/atlas?year=1355&amp;focus=saruhanids">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

